- [revise] Types & Lexicon: grammar `type ::= NUMBER | BOOL | SYMBOL | FRAME | LIST[type] | REF[entity-category]; value ::= NUMBER_LITERAL | BOOL_LITERAL | semantic-symbol | FRAME_LITERAL | list_lit | scoped_ref | diff; FRAME_LITERAL ::= FRAME '(' frame_field (',' frame_field)* ')'; frame_field ::= frame_attribute '=' frame_value; frame_value ::= value | DATE_LITERAL | DATETIME_LITERAL | NAME_LITERAL | URL_LITERAL | PATH_LITERAL; list_lit ::= '[' value (',' value)* ']'; diff ::= VAR_ID '-' NUMBER_LITERAL. All terminals and lexical classes are defined by structural-lexicon, identifier-form, literal-form, semantic-vocabulary, and operation-vocabulary.` — BrainCode has six value types: NUMBER, BOOL, SYMBOL, FRAME, LIST[T], and REF[C]. STRING is removed. SYMBOL values are canonical glossary members. FRAME is an ordered, typed symbolic record whose first field is intent and whose remaining fields occur in the canonical attribute order declared by the intent's glossary entry. REF[C] is an opaque runtime reference to an entity in glossary entity category C; it is not constructible from a symbol. A source expression is statically invalid if any token cannot be traced to a structural member, a descriptive vocabulary member, Identifier Form, Literal Form, or an earlier valid binding. Comments are not part of BrainCode expressions and are excluded from the grammar.
- [revise] Task: grammar `document ::= entrypoint declaration+; entrypoint ::= ENTRYPOINT TASK_ID; declaration ::= task | conversation; task ::= TASK TASK_ID param_list? (':' type)? ('DECREASES' VAR_ID)? '{' step+ return_stmt? '}'; param_list ::= '(' param (',' param)* ')'; param ::= VAR_ID ':' type; return_stmt ::= RETURN value; task_call ::= TASK_ID arg_list? ('->' VAR_ID)?; arg_list ::= '(' arg (',' arg)* ')'; arg ::= argument-attribute '=' value. TASK_ID, VAR_ID, and argument-attribute are glossary-defined forms or members.` — A document has exactly one ENTRYPOINT and it names a zero-parameter TASK_ID or a CONVO declaration. Task and variable names follow Identifier Form rather than carrying English descriptions. Task-call argument attributes are declared in the called task's glossary-visible signature and appear in signature order. CONTEXT execution_date is removed because it relied on a string-shaped date field; a resolved DATE literal is placed directly in the appropriate symbolic frame or operation attribute. All previous typing, lexical scoping, result, return, and termination rules remain, with STRING references replaced by SYMBOL or FRAME as appropriate.
- [revise] Action: grammar `action ::= ACTION operation-symbol '(' operation-arguments? ')' ('->' VAR_ID ':' type)?; operation-arguments ::= operation-argument (',' operation-argument)*; operation-argument ::= operation-attribute '=' action-value; action-value ::= value | REF_VALUE. The Operation Vocabulary entry for operation-symbol uniquely defines the complete ordered operation-attribute sequence, allowed action-value category/type for each attribute, and result type.` — Action is the sole external-state construct. Its operation symbol must be an Operation Vocabulary member and its attributes must exactly match that member's declared ordered signature; arbitrary operations, arbitrary attributes, duplicate attributes, and default signatures are static errors. target, destination, recipient, filters, code changes, messages, and other semantic arguments use entity symbols, role symbols, references, atomic literals, or FRAME values only. content is a FRAME, never source prose. Existing SAME, NEW, MEMBER, VALUE, and NONE result-category semantics remain, but textual VALUE results are FRAME or SYMBOL values according to the operation signature. REF result binding names use Identifier Form and do not encode a hidden natural-language descriptor.
- [revise] Utterance: grammar `utterance ::= UTTER speech-act-symbol '(' utterance-arguments? ')' ('->' VAR_ID ':' FRAME)?; utterance-arguments ::= recipient '=' role-symbol ',' content '=' FRAME_LITERAL | content '=' FRAME_LITERAL; optional utterance fields follow the speech-act-symbol entry's canonical ordered signature.` — UTTER records a pure communicative act and is legal only in a CONVO TURN. The speech-act symbol is a Semantic Vocabulary member, and content is always a FRAME_LITERAL whose intent and fields are defined by the glossary. recipient, tone, audience, format, and other fields are represented as canonical symbols or frame fields; register_note and prose content are removed. A bound utterance result has type FRAME and is available only in the ordinary lexical scope. The utterance signature table determines which fields are valid and their order.
- [revise] Generate: grammar `generation ::= GENERATE '(' target '=' generation-target ',' content '=' FRAME_LITERAL (',' audience '=' role-symbol)? (',' quantity '=' NUMBER_LITERAL)? ')' '->' VAR_ID ':' gen_type; gen_type ::= SYMBOL | FRAME | NUMBER | BOOL | LIST[gen_type].` — GENERATE is a pure operation that produces a value from a symbolic FRAME specification. Its target is a glossary-defined artifact/entity symbol, content is a FRAME_LITERAL, audience is a role symbol, and quantity is an exact NUMBER literal. STRING output and prose-generating instructions are removed. A generated FRAME or SYMBOL is interpreted against the same glossary as all other values; generated output may not introduce an unlisted token. REF types remain prohibited at every nesting depth.
- [revise] Check: grammar `check ::= disjunction; disjunction ::= conjunction (OR conjunction)*; conjunction ::= negation (AND negation)*; negation ::= NOT negation | primary; primary ::= '(' check ')' | quantifier | comparison; comparison ::= comparable-value (comp-op comparable-value)?; comparable-value ::= value. comp-op ::= == | != | < | <= | > | >=.` — Check is a pure typed boolean expression. Values may be glossary members, FRAME values, approved literals, scoped identifiers, references, lists, and defined arithmetic differences; no string literal or unlisted identifier is legal. FRAME equality is structural equality in its canonical field order. Comparison operators and their type restrictions remain as before, except that SYMBOL equality compares canonical symbol identity and FRAME supports only equality and inequality.
- [revise] Flow-If: grammar `flow_if ::= IF check THEN '{' step+ '}' (ELSE '{' step+ '}')?` — Flow-If retains its existing execution, branch-scope, and deferred-query behavior. Its only condition form is the revised closed-vocabulary Check; THEN, ELSE, braces, and IF are Structural Lexicon terminals.
- [revise] Iterate: grammar `flow_for ::= FOR EACH VAR_ID IN iterable '{' step+ '}'; iterable ::= VAR_ID | list_lit; quantifier ::= (ANY | ALL) VAR_ID IN iterable SATISFIES check.` — Iterate retains finite LIST[T] requirements, sequential order, no-shadowing, and narrow quantifier scope. Loop and quantifier variables must use Identifier Form; list elements must be values from the revised closed value grammar. No list may contain a string or an unconstrained identifier.
- [revise] Bind: grammar `bind ::= LET VAR_ID ':' type '=' value; action_result ::= '->' VAR_ID ':' type; task_call_result ::= '->' VAR_ID; utterance_result ::= '->' VAR_ID ':' FRAME; generation_result ::= '->' VAR_ID ':' gen_type.` — Bind remains the common data-flow rule for LET and construct results. VAR_ID must follow Identifier Form's sequential source-order rule. LET may bind only a value in the revised closed vocabulary system; it cannot bind quoted text, an arbitrary word, or a reference manufactured from a semantic symbol. All previous scope, no-shadowing, and static-type rules remain.
- [revise] Conversation: grammar `conversation ::= CONVO TASK_ID '{' turn+ '}'; turn ::= TURN TURN_ID SPEAKER '=' speaker-role (REPLY_TO TURN_ID)? (REVISES TURN_ID)? '{' step+ '}'; speaker-role ::= USER | AGENT.` — Conversation retains ordered turns, independent turn scopes, REPLY_TO adjacency, and linear whole-body REVISES resolution. Conversation and turn names use Identifier Form. SPEAKER values are canonical speaker-role symbols. Every Utterance within a turn uses a closed symbolic speech-act signature and FRAME content; therefore a conversation cannot contain quoted dialogue, fragments, or English sentence payloads.
- [remove] types-lexicon: grammar `None` — None
- [remove] action: grammar `None` — None
- [remove] bind: grammar `None` — None

Task: "add a motherboard under $200 and a compatible processor at any price to the shopping cart."

Return JSON: {"braincode": "..."}

--- RESPONSE ---
{"braincode": "ENTRYPOINT AddMotherboardAndProcessor\n\nTASK AddMotherboardAndProcessor {\n  ACTION search_web(target=\"motherboard\") -> motherboards : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION select_filter(target=motherboards, content=\"price < $200\") -> filtered_motherboards : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION extract(target=filtered_motherboards, limit=1) -> motherboard : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION add_to_cart(target=motherboard)\n  ACTION search_web(target=\"processor compatible with motherboard\") -> processors : LIST[REF[STRING]]\n  ACTION extract(target=processors, limit=1) -> processor : REF[STRING]\n  ACTION add_to_cart(target=processor)\n}"}

